# Test: VPP worker cores come from the kernel's isolated set, and ze doctor
#       reports a placement that does not
# Spec: plan/immediate/spec-vpp-isolated-cpus.md AC-1, AC-5
# VALIDATES: on a host whose kernel isolated cores 2-4, `vpp cpu workers 2`
#            places the workers on isolated cores, so the vpp-cpu-isolation
#            doctor check stays silent (AC-1); a worker core outside the
#            isolated set is reported (AC-5).
# PREVENTS: a busy-polling VPP worker sharing a CPU with the Linux scheduler
#           while the operator believes the isolated cores were used, which is
#           the silent underperformance this spec exists to remove.
#
# This is the end-to-end discrimination for AC-1. The arithmetic this spec
# replaces gave main-core+1 .. main-core+N, so `main-core 0, workers 2` used to
# answer cores 1-2. Core 1 is NOT in the fixture's isolated set, so the first
# command below would report doctor-vpp-cpu-isolation under the old placement
# and reports nothing under the new one.
#
# Linux-only: the fixture stands in for /sys/devices/system/cpu, and the doctor
# check is Linux-tagged. The fixture host has eight CPUs and isolated 2-4.
#
# ONE SCENARIO PER FILE, and that is not style. An expect=stdout: assertion is
# FILE-level: the runner checks it against the concatenated output of every
# command in the file (runner_exec.go, rec.ClientOutput). A file that asserts
# !contains for one command and contains for another asserts two contradictory
# things about one string, and whichever the runner reaches first decides. So
# the case where a worker core is NOT isolated lives in
# test/plugin/vpp-cpu-not-isolated.ci, and only the exit code is per-command.
#
# NOT covered by a .ci, covered by unit tests in the same change: a host whose
# isolated file cannot be read at all reports "does not report which CPUs are
# isolated" rather than a clean state. That needs a second CPU root, and
# option=env carries one value per FILE. It is asserted at the producer by
# TestEvaluateVPPCPUIsolationUnknownBeatsEmpty and
# TestHostCPUInventoryIsolationUnknown
# (internal/component/vpp/doctor_cpu_linux_test.go, cpuset_test.go).

option=skip-os:value=aix,android,darwin,dragonfly,freebsd,illumos,ios,js,netbsd,openbsd,plan9,solaris,wasip1,windows
option=env:var=ze.test.vpp.cpu.root:value=cpufix
# The hugepage check shares this report. Its root is redirected to a directory
# that does not exist, so it reports one error on every vpp-enabled config and
# the exit code below is a fact about the fixture rather than about the machine
# running the test.
option=env:var=ze.test.doctor.hugepages-root:value=nohugepages

tmpfs=cpufix/online:terminator=EOF_ON
0-7
EOF_ON

tmpfs=cpufix/isolated:terminator=EOF_ISO
2-4
EOF_ISO

tmpfs=isolated-workers.conf:terminator=EOF_ISOW
vpp {
	enabled true;
	cpu {
		main-core 0;
		workers 2;
	}
}
EOF_ISOW

tmpfs=vpp-off.conf:terminator=EOF_OFF
vpp {
	enabled false;
	cpu {
		main-core 0;
		workers 2;
	}
}
EOF_OFF

# AC-1: workers land on isolated cores 2 and 3, so nothing is reported.
cmd=foreground:seq=1:exec=ze doctor --json isolated-workers.conf:exit=1
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:!contains=doctor-vpp-cpu-isolation

# VPP disabled: the check is silent whatever the host holds.
cmd=foreground:seq=2:exec=ze doctor --json vpp-off.conf:exit=0
expect=exit:code=0
expect=stdout:!contains=doctor-vpp-cpu-isolation
